  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed development, by virtue of its scale, form, siting, alignment and detailed design would cause harm to the Church Street Conservation Area, which is not outweighed by public benefits, contrary to Section 72 of the Planning (Listed Buildings and Conservation Areas) Act 1990; Sections 12 and 16 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2021); Policies D3, D4, HC1 of the London Plan (2021); CP30 and CP31 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010); DMD37 and DMD44 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014); the Church Street Conservation Area Management proposals (2015); and, the Enfield Heritage Strategy 2019 - 2024 (2019).",
    "The proposed development, by virtue of its bulk, scale, mass, height, siting, excessive footprint, pattern of fenestration, and proximity to the site boundaries would give rise to an unneighbourly loss of privacy, sunlight and daylight, outlook, as perceived from neighbouring properties No's 64 and 66 Church Street including loss of their rear gardens, and overlook the rear garden of No. 62 Church Street and impact on its privacy contrary to Section 12 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policies D3, D4, D6 of the London Plan (2021), CP4 and CP30 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), DMD6, DMD8, DMD10 and DMD37 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014) and the London Housing Supplementary Planning Guidance (2016).",
    "The proposed development, by virtue of the insufficient provision of private amenity space, internal storage space, inadequate  refuse and cycle storage provision, poor quality of outlook, and insufficient natural light, would result in substandard and inappropriate accommodation incapable of meeting the reasonable needs of occupiers, or adhere to the principles of inclusive design contrary to Section 12 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policies D3, D4, D5, D6 of the London Plan (2021), CP30 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), DMD6, DMD8, DMD9 and DMD37 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014) and the London Housing Supplementary Planning Guidance (2016).",
    "The proposed development, in the absence of an adequate arboriculture report, has failed to demonstrate that the proposal would retain and protect trees of visual amenity and biodiversity value within the site contrary to Section 15 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policies G6 and G7 of the London Plan (2021), CP34 and CP36 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), DMD37, DMD79, DMD 80 and DMD81 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014) and the British Standard for Trees in relation to design, demolition and construction (BS 5837:2012)."
  ],
